VALUES ('6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 'secplus-practice-4', 'CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701) Practice Paper 4', 'comptia-sec-plus', ARRAY['Steganography', 'Salting', 'DDoS', 'Watering Hole', 'SASE', 'FIM', 'UEBA', 'GDPR', 'RACI']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 4, 30);

-- ── Domain: General Security Concepts (12% — 2 questions) ──

VALUES ('03ae4e5e-5658-44e6-82c3-bf8d03c1b2fe', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 1, 'A company stores user passwords by hashing them, but two employees who happen to have the identical password "Summer2024!" show identical hash values in the database — meaning a single leaked hash-cracking result instantly reveals both accounts. What technique, applied before hashing, prevents identical passwords from producing identical hashes?');
VALUES ('03ae4e5e-5658-44e6-82c3-bf8d03c1b2fe', '3b20382c-5bb4-463d-96aa-121d6effe2ee', 'Salting adds a unique, per-user random value to each password before it is hashed — because the salt differs per user, even two users with the IDENTICAL plaintext password end up with completely different combined input to the hash function, and therefore different hash outputs, defeating both this specific "identical hash reveals identical password" leak AND precomputed rainbow-table attacks (which rely on hashing common passwords once and reusing the result against many accounts — a unique salt per account defeats this reuse entirely). The salt itself does not need to be kept secret and is typically stored right alongside the hash, since its purpose is uniqueness, not secrecy — verification simply re-combines the stored salt with the attempted password and re-hashes to compare. A stronger hash algorithm alone (option B) does NOT solve this — any deterministic hash function, no matter how cryptographically strong, will still produce the identical output for identical input UNLESS a salt (or similar per-input uniqueness) is introduced first; this is a genuinely important distinction the exam tests specifically. Encryption with a shared key would also produce IDENTICAL ciphertext for identical plaintext under a fixed key (unless using a mode with unique IVs, a related but distinct concept) — and using reversible encryption for passwords is itself the wrong general practice compared to (salted) hashing.');

VALUES ('37abbc72-f22a-4426-a168-c737cc751260', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 2, 'An attacker embeds a secret message within the pixel data of an ordinary-looking image file, such that the image displays normally and the file size/appearance gives no visible indication anything is hidden inside it. What is this technique called?');
VALUES ('37abbc72-f22a-4426-a168-c737cc751260', 'fc290062-4057-4f75-88c4-1364eafab110', 'Steganography is the practice of hiding data within another file or medium such that the existence of hidden content is itself concealed — a common real technique embeds secret data in the least-significant bits of an image''s pixel values, changes imperceptible to the human eye but recoverable with the right extraction tool, so the image looks completely ordinary to anyone viewing it normally. This is a genuinely distinct concept from encryption: encryption scrambles a message''s content so it can''t be READ without the key, but an encrypted file is usually still visibly identifiable AS an encrypted/protected file; steganography instead hides the very FACT that a hidden message exists at all, which is why the two are sometimes combined (encrypt the secret payload, then steganographically hide the encrypted result) for layered protection. Hashing (covered in Paper 2 and earlier this paper) is a one-way integrity-verification function, unrelated to concealment. Tailgating (covered in Paper 2) is an unrelated PHYSICAL social engineering technique. This is a real, well-documented technique — genuinely undetectable-by-casual-inspection data concealment within ordinary-looking files is a real, demonstrated capability, not a myth.');

-- ── Domain: Threats, Vulnerabilities & Mitigations (22% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('b21437a4-d4d6-422f-8d03-78b8ce70fcd2', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 3, 'A company''s public website suddenly receives an overwhelming volume of legitimate-looking HTTP requests from thousands of distinct source IP addresses simultaneously, exhausting server resources and making the site unavailable to real users. What is this attack pattern called, and what distinguishes it from a simple DoS from one source?');
VALUES ('b21437a4-d4d6-422f-8d03-78b8ce70fcd2', 'e22d9fdd-03cb-4eea-aa62-afb33e597e0d', 'A DDoS attack is specifically distinguished by its DISTRIBUTED nature — attack traffic comes from many, often thousands or millions, of distinct sources simultaneously (frequently a botnet of previously compromised devices under the attacker''s control), rather than a single origin. This distribution is precisely what makes DDoS meaningfully harder to mitigate than a basic single-source DoS: simply blocking one offending IP address (a viable, simple fix for single-source DoS) does nothing against an attack arriving from thousands of different addresses at once, requiring different mitigation approaches entirely — rate-based WAF rules (covered in AWS SAA content), traffic-scrubbing services, and services like Shield (covered in AWS SAA content) that specifically absorb and filter this kind of distributed volumetric traffic. DDoS attacks can be volumetric (simply overwhelming available bandwidth/connections) or more targeted at the application layer (exhausting server-side processing resources with fewer, more expensive-to-process requests) — both are real DDoS sub-types, genuinely distinct from SQL injection (a database-query-manipulation vulnerability, covered in Paper 2) or any physical security concern.');

VALUES ('30ab90b1-e6e9-47f0-8b94-a9b2033bc88f', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 4, 'An attacker researches which external websites a target company''s employees frequently visit (e.g., an industry news site), compromises that legitimate third-party site to serve malware, and waits for employees to visit it during their normal browsing — rather than attacking the company''s own systems or sending phishing emails directly. What is this attack called?');
VALUES ('30ab90b1-e6e9-47f0-8b94-a9b2033bc88f', '7dc40f8e-2b4e-49f7-b420-ac15fd5a72ac', 'A watering hole attack takes its name from the predator behavior of waiting at a location prey is known to frequent, rather than actively hunting them directly — the attacker identifies a legitimate third-party site the target group already trusts and regularly visits (an industry forum, a niche news site, a vendor''s portal), compromises THAT site to serve malware or an exploit, and then simply waits for members of the target group to visit it during their normal, routine browsing, at which point they''re compromised without ever having been directly, individually targeted by a phishing email or direct attack. This is effective specifically because it exploits the VICTIMS'' pre-existing trust in a site they already believe to be legitimate and safe, rather than trying to earn trust through a spoofed/fake pretext the way phishing does. Whaling (executive-targeted, covered in Paper 1), vishing (phone-based, covered in Paper 2), and tailgating (physical, covered in Paper 2) are all genuinely different, previously-covered social engineering categories with different mechanisms — none matches this specific "compromise a trusted third party and wait" pattern.');

VALUES ('8f719f98-5166-4311-9982-79bb84ca9048', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 5, 'A company''s legacy industrial control system runs on an operating system the vendor no longer supports or patches, with no compatible replacement currently available, and the system cannot be immediately decommissioned. What compensating security approach is most appropriate given that patching is not an option?');
VALUES ('8f719f98-5166-4311-9982-79bb84ca9048', '86b85f05-5a76-4d2c-a1aa-82b4e1938ee0', 'Compensating controls are exactly the right concept when the IDEAL/primary control (here, patching) genuinely isn''t available — rather than accepting the elevated risk unmitigated, alternative layered defenses reduce risk through OTHER means: network segmentation/isolation (limiting what can actually reach the vulnerable system, ideally an air gap or heavily restricted network path, drawing on concepts covered in earlier Security+ papers), strict access control (minimizing who/what can interact with it), enhanced monitoring specifically watching that system for anomalous activity, and application allowlisting (only permitting known-good processes to run, catching unknown/malicious execution attempts even without a specific patch for the underlying flaw). This is a genuinely common, real-world situation — legacy industrial control systems, medical devices, and similar constrained environments frequently cannot be patched on a normal cadence (or ever again, for end-of-life systems), which is exactly why compensating controls are a standard, recognized risk-management concept rather than a theoretical edge case. Leaving such a system fully unprotected, or treating "destroy it immediately" as the only valid response when the system is still operationally necessary, are both unrealistic, unhelpful extremes that ignore the actual, common constraint being described.');

-- ── Domain: Security Architecture (18% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('3637daa1-9ff3-40c6-a9cf-3767b2ebc3f6', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 6, 'A company with a distributed, remote workforce wants to converge networking and security functions — including Zero Trust access control, secure web gateway filtering, and firewall-as-a-service — into a single, cloud-delivered architecture rather than routing all remote traffic back through a central on-premises data center just to apply security policy. What architectural approach/category does this describe?');
VALUES ('3637daa1-9ff3-40c6-a9cf-3767b2ebc3f6', 'afe8cced-45de-47b4-ac39-3c7fb2d2cd03', 'SASE (Secure Access Service Edge) is specifically the recognized architectural category describing this convergence: rather than a traditional model where remote users'' traffic is backhauled to a central on-premises data center just to pass through a physical security stack (firewall, web filtering appliances), SASE delivers those same security functions — Zero Trust Network Access (applying Zero Trust principles, covered in Paper 1, specifically to remote connectivity), secure web gateway (filtering/inspecting web traffic), firewall-as-a-service, and often CASB (Cloud Access Security Broker) capabilities — from the cloud, close to wherever the user or device actually is, alongside SD-WAN-style networking. This directly matches a distributed remote workforce''s needs better than the legacy hub-and-spoke backhaul model, which adds real latency and doesn''t scale well as remote work grows. A DMZ (covered in Paper 1) is a network SEGMENTATION concept for hosting public-facing servers — a different, narrower architectural pattern, not a converged security-service platform. A honeynet (covered in Paper 2) is a deliberate decoy environment for attracting attackers, entirely unrelated to legitimate remote-access security architecture.');

VALUES ('508399f3-85bc-44b8-ad85-91539f81f733', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 7, 'A security team wants to be alerted immediately if a critical system file (like a core OS binary or a web server''s configuration file) is modified outside of an authorized, scheduled change window — potentially indicating a compromise even if no malware signature is detected. What security technology monitors for exactly this?');
VALUES ('508399f3-85bc-44b8-ad85-91539f81f733', '1cbaa18e-bb41-4392-aa7e-2a19292641af', 'File Integrity Monitoring is specifically designed for this exact need: it establishes a cryptographic-hash-based (or similar) baseline of designated critical files, then re-checks them (continuously or on a schedule) to detect ANY change — since even a single-bit modification changes a file''s hash entirely (the same integrity principle covered earlier this paper for password/data integrity), FIM can flag an unauthorized change WITHOUT needing to recognize the specific malware or technique that made it, catching genuinely novel or signature-evading compromises that a purely signature-based tool might miss. This makes FIM a valuable, complementary layer alongside EDR (covered in Paper 1, which focuses on process/behavioral monitoring) and other detection tools, specifically for the "did a critical, rarely-changed file get modified outside an authorized window" question. A standard firewall filters network traffic by IP/port/protocol (covered in Paper 1) with no file-content-monitoring capability of its own. A VPN provides encrypted connectivity (covered in Paper 2), also unrelated to file integrity monitoring — both are common category-confusion distractors against a more specialized, purpose-built tool like FIM.');

VALUES ('505ba1a6-943b-43b6-9ca6-e8b3c75e14aa', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 8, 'A company wants to detect a compromised legitimate user account being misused by an attacker — for example, an employee''s account suddenly downloading an unusually large volume of files at 3 AM, a time and behavior pattern wildly inconsistent with that employee''s normal usage — even though the attacker is using genuinely valid, stolen credentials that pass normal authentication. What security technology category is designed to catch this kind of anomalous BEHAVIOR from an otherwise-legitimate account?');
VALUES ('505ba1a6-943b-43b6-9ca6-e8b3c75e14aa', 'fbc42e65-e703-452f-9c52-0fa53af5d1c9', 'UEBA is specifically designed to address exactly this gap: traditional authentication (even MFA, covered in Paper 1) only verifies identity AT THE MOMENT of login — once an attacker has valid, genuinely-working stolen credentials (and has passed MFA, e.g., via a successful phishing of the second factor too, or a compromised device already trusted), authentication alone has no ongoing visibility into what that "authenticated" session actually does afterward. UEBA instead builds an individual behavioral baseline per user/entity (typical login times, typical data volumes accessed, typical systems touched) using machine learning, and flags significant deviations from THAT SPECIFIC baseline — a 3 AM mass file download from an employee who has never worked outside 9-5 business hours is exactly the kind of statistically anomalous behavior UEBA is built to catch, independent of whether the credentials used were technically "valid." MFA (covered in Paper 1) protects the login MOMENT specifically — it has no ongoing post-authentication behavioral monitoring function of its own. A firewall filters network traffic (covered in Paper 1), with no user-behavior-baselining capability. This is a real, meaningful detection capability specifically because authentication alone genuinely cannot catch this class of threat — that''s precisely the gap UEBA exists to fill.');

-- ── Domain: Security Operations (28% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('9763cded-ba3d-4980-9407-804a10b057b0', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 9, 'During an active incident investigation, an analyst needs to capture the contents of a compromised server''s RAM before it is powered off, since RAM contains running processes, network connections, and potentially decrypted data that will be permanently lost the instant power is cut. What forensic principle does this illustrate, and in what order should evidence generally be collected relative to other, more persistent evidence (like disk contents)?');
VALUES ('9763cded-ba3d-4980-9407-804a10b057b0', 'd40845f5-4e35-4956-807b-48466d0ce760', 'Order of volatility is a foundational digital forensics principle specifically because different types of evidence have very different lifespans: RAM contents (running processes, open network connections, encryption keys that may only exist in memory, unsaved data) vanish the instant power is lost, making it the single MOST volatile and time-sensitive evidence type to capture, while data written to disk generally persists even after a clean shutdown, making it comparatively far less time-pressured to collect. The correct general order proceeds from most volatile to least: RAM/running state first, then network connections/logs still in flux, then progressively more persistent evidence like disk contents and archived logs — this directly connects to chain of custody (covered in Paper 1), since evidence must be properly documented and preserved at each collection step regardless of order, but volatility determines WHICH evidence needs to be captured most urgently before it''s irrecoverably lost. Powering off a compromised machine immediately (a common, well-intentioned but often mistaken first instinct) would destroy exactly the RAM-resident evidence this principle exists to preserve — disk data, being comparatively persistent, does NOT need to be collected before the far-more-perishable RAM contents, the reverse of what one distractor option claims.');

VALUES ('5602fdb1-4654-4ce4-b71b-73e022a78c36', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 10, 'A company runs a tabletop exercise simulating a ransomware incident, walking key stakeholders (IT, legal, communications, executive leadership) through a discussion of how they would respond — without actually touching any real production system. What is the value of this exercise, and how does it differ from a live incident response drill involving real systems?');
VALUES ('5602fdb1-4654-4ce4-b71b-73e022a78c36', '26021ab5-6b3e-4286-aac1-2b7149920594', 'A tabletop exercise is specifically a DISCUSSION-based walkthrough — stakeholders talk through "what would we actually do at each stage of this simulated incident" (who gets notified, who makes which decisions, what''s the actual communication plan to customers/regulators, is the backup restoration plan realistic) without touching or disrupting any real system at all, making it low-cost, low-risk, and easy to run relatively frequently. This genuinely reveals real gaps that a purely written incident response PLAN can hide — for example, discovering during the exercise that nobody in the room is actually sure who has legal authority to approve a ransom payment, or that the "backup restoration" step assumes a person who left the company six months ago. A live/technical drill, by contrast, actually exercises real tooling and (often non-production or isolated) systems — more realistic in a different, complementary way, but also more costly, disruptive, and complex to arrange, which is exactly why organizations typically run BOTH types at different cadences rather than treating them as interchangeable. Tabletop exercises are a real, standard, valuable, and frequently recommended practice specifically because they''re cheap enough to run often — dismissing them as valueless misses their actual purpose.');

VALUES ('d33af121-5b92-48fb-8be5-29d6f3c3da41', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 11, 'A security operations team wants to reduce the manual, repetitive effort of responding to common, well-understood alert types (e.g., automatically blocking an IP after 3 confirmed brute-force attempts, or automatically isolating an endpoint flagged by EDR with high confidence) so analysts can focus on genuinely novel or ambiguous incidents instead. What technology category is designed for this kind of automated response?');
VALUES ('d33af121-5b92-48fb-8be5-29d6f3c3da41', 'f20471c3-8aec-486d-b25d-847d14325ff9', 'SOAR platforms are specifically built for this automation need: security teams define "playbooks" — predefined sequences of automated actions triggered by specific alert conditions (often sourced from a SIEM, covered in Paper 1, or EDR, also covered in Paper 1) — so that well-understood, high-confidence, repetitive scenarios (a confirmed brute-force pattern, a high-confidence malware detection) get handled automatically (blocking the source, isolating the endpoint, opening a ticket) WITHOUT requiring a human analyst to manually execute the same routine steps every single time, freeing analyst attention for genuinely novel, ambiguous, or high-stakes situations that actually need human judgment. This directly complements (and often integrates with) the alert-fatigue problem covered in Paper 2 — automating the routine, well-understood cases is itself a mitigation for analysts being overwhelmed by high alert volume. A standard firewall (covered in Paper 1) enforces network rules but has no cross-tool orchestration/playbook-automation capability of its own. AWS KMS manages encryption keys, entirely unrelated to SOC response automation — this IS a real, purpose-built, and increasingly standard SOC technology category specifically because manual response to every routine alert does not scale.');

VALUES ('c4d2896b-1f2f-4f91-bec8-702de220c579', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 12, 'A company detects an active, ongoing intrusion and must decide between two competing priorities: immediately shutting down the attacker''s access (containment) versus continuing to observe the attacker''s activity for a period to gather more intelligence on their methods and objectives before acting. What factors should drive this decision?');
VALUES ('c4d2896b-1f2f-4f91-bec8-702de220c579', '6a583ced-21b3-4f48-b53a-c9ce590a28b9', 'This is a genuine, real operational tension in incident response with no single universally-correct default: immediate containment (covered as the first real incident-response phase in Paper 1) minimizes ongoing damage and data exposure risk the longer an attacker remains active, but can also tip off a sophisticated attacker that they''ve been detected (potentially causing them to destroy evidence, deploy a more destructive payload, or otherwise escalate), and cuts off the opportunity to observe their full toolset, objectives, and the true scope of what they accessed. Measured observation (sometimes done via a controlled, monitored environment) can reveal genuinely valuable intelligence — confirming exactly what data was actually touched, understanding the attacker''s specific techniques for future defense — but extends the window of real, ongoing risk while it continues. The correct answer is that this decision genuinely depends on the specific situation''s severity, the sensitivity of what''s at risk, and organizational risk tolerance, and should be made deliberately by incident response leadership (often informed by legal/regulatory considerations too) rather than following a rigid, one-size-fits-all rule in either direction — treating either "always contain instantly" or "always observe indefinitely" as universally correct oversimplifies a decision real incident responders genuinely have to weigh case by case.');

-- ── Domain: Security Program Management & Oversight (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('1f6e47cc-0a65-48b9-81c5-02858931b5ea', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 13, 'A company handling personal data of European Union residents, even though the company itself is based outside the EU, discovers this may still trigger legal obligations under a specific EU data protection regulation. What is this regulation called, and why does it apply despite the company having no EU physical presence?');
VALUES ('1f6e47cc-0a65-48b9-81c5-02858931b5ea', 'ad5cc7bb-a0d8-43f5-bcc4-4c2e30605c0f', 'GDPR is specifically designed with extraterritorial scope — it applies based on WHOSE data is being processed (EU residents'' personal data) and in what context (e.g., offering goods/services to those residents, or monitoring their behavior), not based on where the processing ORGANIZATION happens to be physically headquartered. This is precisely why a company with zero physical EU presence can still have real, enforceable GDPR obligations (and face real penalties) if they handle EU residents'' personal data — a genuinely important, frequently misunderstood point, since "we''re not based in the EU" is not a valid basis for assuming GDPR doesn''t apply. HIPAA (covered in Paper 1) is specifically a US law governing health information — not an EU regulation, and PCI-DSS (covered in Paper 1) governs payment card data specifically, industry-standard rather than government/EU regulation, and neither is scoped to EU residents'' personal data generally the way GDPR specifically is — a common cross-framework confusion this question directly tests.');

VALUES ('564eff40-1c97-413b-9fa7-2f282e1eed38', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 14, 'A security project involves multiple teams, and there is confusion about who is actually accountable for a decision, who should be consulted before it''s made, and who just needs to be kept informed afterward. What governance tool clarifies these distinct roles for a given task or decision?');
VALUES ('564eff40-1c97-413b-9fa7-2f282e1eed38', '7017a93d-332e-45fe-88a6-5d0756e7af49', 'A RACI matrix is specifically designed to eliminate exactly this kind of cross-team role ambiguity by explicitly naming, for each task or decision: who is RESPONSIBLE (does the actual work), who is ACCOUNTABLE (owns the outcome and has final sign-off authority — critically, this should generally be exactly ONE person/role per task, to avoid diffused, unclear ownership), who must be CONSULTED (their input is sought before the decision, typically subject-matter experts or affected stakeholders), and who is merely INFORMED (notified of the outcome, without needing to provide input beforehand). This is a genuinely common, real project-governance tool used well beyond security specifically, and applies directly to security program management when multiple teams (security, IT, legal, business units) share responsibility for a decision or control. A BIA (covered in Paper 2) analyzes business-function criticality/impact — an entirely different tool with a different purpose. An AUP (covered in Paper 2) defines acceptable employee IT usage behavior, also unrelated to project role/accountability assignment.');

-- ── Domain: Threats, Vulnerabilities & Mitigations (additional — question 15) ──

VALUES ('4dc6e807-dad0-427b-949c-6701e1650f76', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 15, 'An attacker sets up a rogue wireless access point broadcasting the exact same SSID as a coffee shop''s legitimate free Wi-Fi, hoping nearby customers'' devices will connect to the attacker''s access point instead of the real one, letting the attacker intercept their traffic. What is this specific wireless attack called?');
VALUES ('4dc6e807-dad0-427b-949c-6701e1650f76', 'f4df0c92-0bf9-4f97-be62-7f6c3f389257', 'An evil twin attack works specifically by exploiting the fact that most devices connect to a wireless network based on its SSID (broadcast name) and, in many configurations, will automatically reconnect to any network sharing an SSID they''ve previously trusted, with no reliable way for an ordinary user to visually distinguish the attacker''s access point from the legitimate one just by name. Once a victim''s device connects to the evil twin instead of the real access point, the attacker sits in the middle of that traffic, able to intercept unencrypted data, redirect to phishing pages, or attempt further compromise — a genuinely real, well-documented public Wi-Fi attack pattern, which is exactly why using a VPN (covered in Paper 2) on untrusted public networks is a standard, meaningful mitigation regardless of which specific access point a device actually connects to. SQL injection (covered in Paper 2) is an entirely unrelated database-query-manipulation vulnerability, and a watering hole attack (covered earlier this paper) involves compromising a legitimate third-party website, not broadcasting a wireless signal — this is a real and common enough technique that dismissing it as impossible would be a genuine security gap in judgment.');

-- ── Domain: Security Architecture (additional — question 16) ──

VALUES ('fdd18355-0709-4ad8-8bab-f13cd10c0b07', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 16, 'A company wants to ensure that if its primary data center loses power entirely, critical servers keep running long enough for either power to be restored or for an orderly shutdown to occur, without any interruption at the moment power is lost. What single piece of infrastructure directly provides this immediate, bridge-the-gap protection?');
VALUES ('fdd18355-0709-4ad8-8bab-f13cd10c0b07', '1659dee2-5b77-48b2-a326-f638bd36e835', 'A UPS is specifically built to eliminate the interruption GAP at the exact moment utility power fails — its battery is already live and connected, so it takes over supplying power instantly (effectively zero perceptible downtime to connected equipment), which is the critical distinction from a generator: a generator provides genuinely sustainable longer-term backup power, but it takes real time (commonly on the order of seconds to roughly a minute) to start and reach stable output, during which connected equipment would lose power entirely without something bridging that specific gap. This is exactly why the standard, resilient architecture layers BOTH together — UPS for the immediate, zero-gap transition, and a generator taking over from the UPS for anything beyond a brief outage the UPS''s limited battery capacity can sustain on its own — rather than treating either as a complete solution by itself. A firewall (covered in Paper 1) filters network traffic with no power-continuity function, and a VPN concentrator (a VPN-endpoint device) is likewise unrelated to physical power redundancy — this IS a real, standard, widely deployed piece of physical security/resilience infrastructure, not a theoretical impossibility.');

-- ── Domain: Security Operations (additional — questions 17-18) ──

VALUES ('39797df0-45ce-49d1-bf0c-007aaec16854', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 17, 'A company wants a written, standardized process describing exactly how to respond to a specific, recurring type of security event (for example, "a laptop is reported lost or stolen") so that any analyst on any shift handles it the same, complete way every time, rather than improvising from memory. What is this kind of document called?');
VALUES ('39797df0-45ce-49d1-bf0c-007aaec16854', '19811cb5-e3b9-43ce-a1b9-1427436bf9db', 'A playbook (sometimes called a runbook) is specifically a concrete, step-by-step written procedure scoped to ONE particular, recurring scenario — "lost/stolen laptop," "confirmed phishing click," "ransomware detected on an endpoint" — so that the correct, complete sequence of actions (who to notify, what to disable/revoke, what evidence to preserve) happens the same way regardless of which analyst is handling it or what shift it occurs on, reducing the risk of a step being forgotten under the stress of an active incident. This sits at a different, more concrete level than a broader incident response POLICY (which establishes overall authority, goals, and scope — covered conceptually in earlier papers'' incident-response-lifecycle questions) or a general incident response PLAN (the overall lifecycle framework) — playbooks are the specific, actionable "do this, then this, then this" procedures that operationalize that broader plan for individual scenario types, and directly connect to the SOAR automation concept covered earlier this paper, since SOAR platforms specifically execute AUTOMATED playbooks built on this same underlying concept. An AUP (covered in Paper 2) governs acceptable employee IT usage, and a BIA (covered in Paper 2) analyzes business-function criticality — neither is a step-by-step incident-response procedure document.');

VALUES ('f45f14b2-b8e0-4423-af39-8bdc63a2ffec', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 18, 'After fully resolving a security incident, the response team holds a structured meeting to document exactly what happened, what worked well, what didn''t, and what changes should be made to prevent a recurrence — deliberately without assigning individual blame, so that people are honest about mistakes rather than defensive. What is this final incident-response activity called?');
VALUES ('f45f14b2-b8e0-4423-af39-8bdc63a2ffec', 'c887d74e-b243-4611-b8ed-569c4779a135', 'A lessons-learned review (also called a post-incident review or retrospective) is specifically the FINAL phase of the incident response lifecycle, occurring only after the incident is fully resolved — distinct from containment and eradication (earlier lifecycle phases, covered conceptually in prior papers'' incident-response-order questions, which happen DURING active response, not after). Its deliberately blameless framing is a genuinely important, specific characteristic: if participants fear individual blame or punishment for admitting a mistake (a missed alert, a delayed response step), they''re far less likely to be fully honest about what actually happened, which directly undermines the review''s entire purpose of surfacing REAL, actionable gaps — a blameless culture is what makes people willing to say "I saw that alert and dismissed it as noise" instead of hiding it. The concrete output is typically specific, actionable changes: updating a playbook (the concept covered immediately prior in this paper), adjusting detection rules, or closing a process gap the incident revealed — this is a real, standard, and valuable final step precisely because incidents recur in similar forms, and skipping this step means repeating the same preventable mistakes.');

-- ── Domain: Security Program Management & Oversight (additional — questions 19-20) ──

VALUES ('5d8d872f-e4b7-4789-b0fe-82f4849f07b8', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 19, 'A company wants to formally verify that a specific vendor''s security practices meet an acceptable standard BEFORE signing a contract giving that vendor access to company data — rather than simply trusting the vendor''s own marketing claims about their security posture. What process addresses this?');
VALUES ('5d8d872f-e4b7-4789-b0fe-82f4849f07b8', '7b170d47-b237-4a0f-a45d-914dd2c239a5', 'Vendor risk assessment is specifically the recognized process for this exact need — since a company''s own security posture is only as strong as the weakest third party it grants data/system access to, formally evaluating a prospective vendor BEFORE signing (reviewing independent audit reports such as a SOC 2, checking for relevant security certifications, running a structured security questionnaire, or in higher-risk cases a direct audit) provides real, evidence-based verification rather than simply trusting the vendor''s own self-reported marketing claims about their security. This is typically an ongoing discipline, not purely a one-time, pre-signing gate — recurring reassessment (a distinct concept covered in Paper 2) continues checking that a vendor''s practices remain acceptable throughout the relationship, since a vendor''s security posture can genuinely degrade or change after the initial contract is signed. A tabletop exercise (covered earlier this paper) is an internal incident-response-plan rehearsal, unrelated to vendor evaluation, and FIM (also covered earlier this paper) monitors file changes on a company''s own systems — neither substitutes for actually verifying a third party''s security practices before trusting them with data access.');

VALUES ('a7180e92-c5e3-4087-9497-0e18f9e18cd9', '6bad4f42-b451-4924-9a26-c7f686af8fbe', 20, 'A company''s security policy states that any new system deployed into production must first pass a documented security review, but a business unit deploys a new internal tool without going through that review because they felt the deadline pressure justified skipping it. What governance concept describes deviating from an established, documented policy like this, and what should happen as a result?');
VALUES ('a7180e92-c5e3-4087-9497-0e18f9e18cd9', '807f077b-51ad-46bf-9195-bbb191630a80', 'A deviation from documented policy is a genuine governance event that needs a real process, not something to quietly ignore OR treat as automatically fine because of business pressure: the correct, mature governance approach is a FORMAL exception process — the requesting team documents WHY the deviation is needed, the risk it introduces is actually assessed, an appropriate authority (often security leadership, not the requesting business unit itself) reviews and either approves (frequently requiring compensating controls, covered earlier this paper, to offset the skipped control, plus a defined expiration/review date rather than an open-ended permanent exception) or denies it. If a deviation already happened WITHOUT going through that process (as in this scenario), governance/oversight should identify and address it after the fact — through the same risk-assessment lens, deciding whether to retroactively formalize an exception with compensating controls, or require the review be completed before the tool continues running in production. Treating deadline pressure as an automatic, permanent override — or treating documented policy as purely aspirational and never actually enforced — both undermine the entire point of having a documented security policy in the first place, which is exactly why formal exception governance exists as the real, standard middle path between "rigid, no-exceptions-ever" and "policy means nothing under pressure."');
